WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.409
Entity Framework Core helps reduce injection risk when developers use LINQ and parameterized APIs as intended.

2
00:00:07.409 --> 00:00:15.289
In common query patterns, EF Core translates expression trees and values into provider-supported database operations.

3
00:00:15.289 --> 00:00:23.977
That is a strong baseline for many applications, especially compared with handwritten command text assembled from request values.

4
00:00:23.977 --> 00:00:29.971
EF Core does not remove the need for authorization, validation, data shaping, and review.

5
00:00:29.971 --> 00:00:35.831
A query can be safely parameterized and still return records the caller should not see.

6
00:00:35.831 --> 00:00:40.208
A model can be mapped correctly and still expose too many fields.

7
00:00:40.208 --> 00:00:46.068
A filter can be syntactically safe and still allow an inappropriate business operation.

8
00:00:46.068 --> 00:00:51.456
Injection prevention is necessary, but not the whole data-access security story.

9
00:00:51.456 --> 00:00:54.217
Raw SQL features are higher-review areas.

10
00:00:54.217 --> 00:01:09.102
EF Core supports raw SQL for legitimate reasons such as performance, provider-specific capabilities, or complex queries, but those features should preserve parameterization and keep variable data out of command structure.

11
00:01:09.102 --> 00:01:15.837
A raw SQL path should have a clear reason, an owner, and review proportional to the data it touches.

12
00:01:15.837 --> 00:01:20.282
Dynamic filtering, sorting, and projection require careful design.

13
00:01:20.282 --> 00:01:26.141
Callers should not provide arbitrary field names, query fragments, or projection rules.

14
00:01:26.141 --> 00:01:34.762
Instead, the application should map supported sort fields, filters, report columns, and views to approved internal query shapes.

15
00:01:34.762 --> 00:01:41.026
This preserves flexibility for clients while keeping SQL structure under application control.

16
00:01:41.026 --> 00:01:46.010
Separating API boundary models from persistence models can improve safety.

17
00:01:46.010 --> 00:01:50.455
Request DTOs should express what the caller is allowed to provide.

18
00:01:50.455 --> 00:01:54.967
Response DTOs should express what the caller is allowed to receive.

19
00:01:54.967 --> 00:02:05.070
Database entities often contain relationships, audit fields, internal flags, or operational data that should not become external contract by accident.

20
00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:08.909
Performance-driven query changes deserve security review.

21
00:02:08.909 --> 00:02:16.587
Optimization work may introduce raw SQL, broader projections, caching, denormalized views, or reporting shortcuts.

22
00:02:16.587 --> 00:02:26.286
Those changes can be valuable, but they should still preserve parameterization, authorization filters, data minimization, and tenant boundaries.
